  "messages": [
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "building on idea",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.4646,
      "sentimentLabel": "neutral",
      "sentimentScore": 0.5,
      "sequenceNumber": 1,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "So we need to choose between Trie, HashMap, and Sorted Array for search autocomplete with 1 million words.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:00:05.000Z",
      "userId": "user_emma",
      "userName": "Emma"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "building on idea",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.6895,
      "sentimentLabel": "neutral",
      "sentimentScore": 0.5,
      "sequenceNumber": 2,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "I think Trie is the obvious choice for autocomplete. It's literally designed for prefix matching.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:00:20.000Z",
      "userId": "user_liam",
      "userName": "Liam"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "building on idea",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.8956,
      "sentimentLabel": "neutral",
      "sentimentScore": 0.5,
      "sequenceNumber": 3,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "Trie makes sense. Each node represents a character, and we traverse until we find all completions.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:00:38.000Z",
      "userId": "user_sofia",
      "userName": "Sofia"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "building on idea",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.752,
      "sentimentLabel": "positive",
      "sentimentScore": 0.85,
      "sequenceNumber": 4,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "Yeah Trie is good for this. Prefix lookup is O(m) where m is the prefix length.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:00:55.000Z",
      "userId": "user_noah",
      "userName": "Noah"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "building on idea",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.4517,
      "sentimentLabel": "neutral",
      "sentimentScore": 0.58,
      "sequenceNumber": 5,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "But wait, with 1 million words, the memory usage of a Trie could be really high. Each node has up to 26 children.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:01:15.000Z",
      "userId": "user_emma",
      "userName": "Emma"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 1,
      "messageType": "disagreement",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.3362,
      "sentimentLabel": "positive",
      "sentimentScore": 0.6062,
      "sequenceNumber": 6,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "That's true but Tries are still good. We can optimize with compressed tries or radix trees.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:01:35.000Z",
      "userId": "user_liam",
      "userName": "Liam"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 1,
      "messageType": "question",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.5508,
      "sentimentLabel": "neutral",
      "sentimentScore": 0.55,
      "sequenceNumber": 7,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "Compressed tries help with memory. But is the implementation complexity worth it?",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:01:55.000Z",
      "userId": "user_sofia",
      "userName": "Sofia"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "disagreement",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.8291,
      "sentimentLabel": "positive",
      "sentimentScore": 0.6361,
      "sequenceNumber": 8,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "I still think a regular Trie is fine. Memory is cheap. The prefix lookup speed is what matters.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:02:15.000Z",
      "userId": "user_noah",
      "userName": "Noah"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "question",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.425,
      "sentimentLabel": "positive",
      "sentimentScore": 0.6969,
      "sequenceNumber": 9,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "But are we sure Trie is the best? We haven't really considered the other options yet.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:02:35.000Z",
      "userId": "user_emma",
      "userName": "Emma"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "question",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.6068,
      "sentimentLabel": "neutral",
      "sentimentScore": 0.5312,
      "sequenceNumber": 10,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "Trie is designed for this exact use case. Why would we need to look at other options?",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:02:55.000Z",
      "userId": "user_liam",
      "userName": "Liam"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "building on idea",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.445,
      "sentimentLabel": "neutral",
      "sentimentScore": 0.475,
      "sequenceNumber": 11,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "Liam has a point. Tries are the standard for autocomplete.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:03:12.000Z",
      "userId": "user_sofia",
      "userName": "Sofia"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "disagreement",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.3953,
      "sentimentLabel": "positive",
      "sentimentScore": 0.6429,
      "sequenceNumber": 12,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "Yeah, I think we should just go with Trie. It's the right tool for the job.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:03:30.000Z",
      "userId": "user_noah",
      "userName": "Noah"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": -1,
      "messageType": "building on idea",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.7225,
      "sentimentLabel": "positive",
      "sentimentScore": 0.75,
      "sequenceNumber": 13,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "OK so Trie it is. Let's write the implementation. Insert and search methods.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:03:48.000Z",
      "userId": "user_liam",
      "userName": "Liam"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": -1,
      "messageType": "question",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.4758,
      "sentimentLabel": "neutral",
      "sentimentScore": 0.5,
      "sequenceNumber": 14,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "I guess. But the question asked us to discuss tradeoffs between all three options.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:04:05.000Z",
      "userId": "user_emma",
      "userName": "Emma"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "disagreement",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.4424,
      "sentimentLabel": "neutral",
      "sentimentScore": 0.575,
      "sequenceNumber": 15,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "The tradeoffs are obvious. Trie wins on prefix search. HashMap is O(1) but can't do prefix. Sorted array needs binary search.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:04:25.000Z",
      "userId": "user_liam",
      "userName": "Liam"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "disagreement",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.3686,
      "sentimentLabel": "positive",
      "sentimentScore": 0.6429,
      "sequenceNumber": 16,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "Right, HashMap can't do prefix matching at all without iterating everything.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:04:42.000Z",
      "userId": "user_sofia",
      "userName": "Sofia"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "building on idea",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.4255,
      "sentimentLabel": "neutral",
      "sentimentScore": 0.5,
      "sequenceNumber": 17,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "And sorted array with binary search is O(log n) for finding the start but then linear for collecting results.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:04:58.000Z",
      "userId": "user_noah",
      "userName": "Noah"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "question",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.4251,
      "sentimentLabel": "neutral",
      "sentimentScore": 0.55,
      "sequenceNumber": 18,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "So Trie is clearly the winner. Can we move on to implementation?",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:05:15.000Z",
      "userId": "user_liam",
      "userName": "Liam"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "new idea",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.4717,
      "sentimentLabel": "neutral",
      "sentimentScore": 0.5,
      "sequenceNumber": 19,
      "source": "voice",
      "text": "Wait, I just thought of something. What about a HashMap where keys are all possible prefixes? That would give O(1) prefix lookup.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:05:50.000Z",
      "userId": "user_emma",
      "userName": "Emma"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "building on idea",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.808,
      "sentimentLabel": "positive",
      "sentimentScore": 0.8125,
      "sequenceNumber": 20,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "Oh that's interesting! Pre-compute all prefixes and map them to lists of completions.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:06:10.000Z",
      "userId": "user_noah",
      "userName": "Noah"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "building on idea",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.4405,
      "sentimentLabel": "positive",
      "sentimentScore": 0.6667,
      "sequenceNumber": 21,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "That would use more memory but lookups would be constant time. With 1M words, how many prefixes would there be?",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:06:30.000Z",
      "userId": "user_sofia",
      "userName": "Sofia"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "building on idea",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.611,
      "sentimentLabel": "neutral",
      "sentimentScore": 0.425,
      "sequenceNumber": 22,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "If average word length is 8 characters, that's about 8 million prefix entries. Each pointing to a list of completions.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:06:50.000Z",
      "userId": "user_emma",
      "userName": "Emma"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "disagreement",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.4887,
      "sentimentLabel": "positive",
      "sentimentScore": 0.6111,
      "sequenceNumber": 23,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "OK I take back what I said. The prefix-HashMap approach is actually really clever for a read-heavy system.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:07:10.000Z",
      "userId": "user_liam",
      "userName": "Liam"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "building on idea",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.5017,
      "sentimentLabel": "neutral",
      "sentimentScore": 0.5,
      "sequenceNumber": 24,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "Yeah, autocomplete is definitely read-heavy. You build the index once and serve millions of queries.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:07:30.000Z",
      "userId": "user_noah",
      "userName": "Noah"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "disagreement",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.5215,
      "sentimentLabel": "neutral",
      "sentimentScore": 0.5833,
      "sequenceNumber": 25,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "So the tradeoff is: Trie uses less memory but Trie traversal per query, HashMap uses more memory but O(1) per query.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:07:50.000Z",
      "userId": "user_sofia",
      "userName": "Sofia"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "building on idea",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.4887,
      "sentimentLabel": "positive",
      "sentimentScore": 0.6167,
      "sequenceNumber": 26,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "And sorted array is the middle ground — moderate memory, O(log n + k) lookup. Good for memory-constrained environments.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:08:10.000Z",
      "userId": "user_emma",
      "userName": "Emma"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": -1,
      "messageType": "building on idea",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.4728,
      "sentimentLabel": "positive",
      "sentimentScore": 0.75,
      "sequenceNumber": 27,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "This is a much better analysis now. Each approach has legitimate use cases depending on constraints.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:08:30.000Z",
      "userId": "user_liam",
      "userName": "Liam"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "building on idea",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.558,
      "sentimentLabel": "positive",
      "sentimentScore": 1.0,
      "sequenceNumber": 28,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "For a cloud service with plenty of RAM, the prefix-HashMap is probably best. For mobile or embedded, Trie or sorted array.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:08:50.000Z",
      "userId": "user_noah",
      "userName": "Noah"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "question",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.4153,
      "sentimentLabel": "positive",
      "sentimentScore": 0.625,
      "sequenceNumber": 29,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "Should we also discuss the sorted array approach more? With binary search to find lower and upper bounds of the prefix?",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:09:10.000Z",
      "userId": "user_sofia",
      "userName": "Sofia"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "building on idea",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.4775,
      "sentimentLabel": "neutral",
      "sentimentScore": 0.5625,
      "sequenceNumber": 30,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "Yes! Java's Arrays.binarySearch or C++'s lower_bound. You find the first word starting with the prefix and iterate until the prefix no longer matches.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:09:30.000Z",
      "userId": "user_emma",
      "userName": "Emma"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "building on idea",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.7547,
      "sentimentLabel": "positive",
      "sentimentScore": 0.625,
      "sequenceNumber": 31,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "That's elegant. And the memory overhead is just the array itself — no extra data structure. Smallest memory footprint.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:09:48.000Z",
      "userId": "user_liam",
      "userName": "Liam"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": -1,
      "messageType": "building on idea",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.376,
      "sentimentLabel": "positive",
      "sentimentScore": 0.6652,
      "sequenceNumber": 32,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "Great, so our recommendation should be context-dependent. No single right answer — that's the whole point of the problem.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:10:05.000Z",
      "userId": "user_noah",
      "userName": "Noah"
    },
    {
      "clusterLabel": 0,
      "messageType": "disagreement",
      "messageTypeConfidence": 0.2469,
      "sentimentLabel": "positive",
      "sentimentScore": 0.7,
      "sequenceNumber": 33,
      "source": "text",
      "text": "Nice recovery team. We almost just picked Trie without thinking. Emma's HashMap idea really opened up the discussion.",
      "timestamp": "2026-03-15T16:10:20.000Z",
      "userId": "user_sofia",
      "userName": "Sofia"
    }
  ],
